wajo23_q2.solution:
The 18 players of one team shook hands with each of the 18 players of the other team, and the 3 umpires shook hands with each of 36 players, which is<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
18 \cdot 18+36 \cdot 3 &amp; =324+108 \\
&amp; =432 \text{ handshakes. }
\end{aligned}
$$

wajo23_q3.solution:
Noting that a number is divisible by 3, if its digit sum is divisible by 3; and a number is divisible by 11, if its alternating sum is divisible by 11, we see that: <br>$3 \mid 123321$, since $3 \mid 12=1+2+3+3+2+1$, and <br>$11 \mid 23321$, since $11 \mid 0=-1+2-3+3-2+1$.<br>Therefore,<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
123321 &amp; =3 \cdot 41107 \\
&amp; =3 \cdot 11 \cdot 3737 \\
&amp; =3 \cdot 11 \cdot 37 \cdot 101 \text{ (prime factorisation). }
\end{aligned}
$$<br>So the largest prime factor of 123321 is 101.

wajo23_q5.solution:
Below, we use the overline notation to refer to the decimal representation of a number, so that $\overline{a b c}=100 a+10 b+c$.<br>Divisibility by 12 is equivalent to divisibility by both 3 and 4.<br>A number is divisible by 3, if and only if its digit sum is divisible by 3.<br>A number is divisible by 4, if and only the number formed by its last two digits is divisible by 4. So,<br>$$
12|\overline{a b c} \Longleftrightarrow 3|(a+b+c) \text{ and } 4 \mid \overline{b c}
$$<br>Now $4 \mid \overline{b c}$ for $\overline{b c} \in\{12,16,32,36,52,56,72,76,92,96\}$, or in other words,<br>$$
c \in\{2,6\} \text{ for any of the } 5 \text{ odd choices of } b \in\{1,3,5,7,9\}.
$$<br>After having chosen $b$ and $c$, we need only ensure $a$ is chosen such that $3 \mid a+b+c$. Now,<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
&amp; b+c \equiv 0 \quad(\bmod 3) \Longrightarrow a \in\{3,6,9\} \\
&amp; b+c \equiv 1 \quad(\bmod 3) \Longrightarrow a \in\{2,5,8\} \\
&amp; b+c \equiv 2 \quad(\bmod 3) \Longrightarrow a \in\{1,4,7\}
\end{aligned}
$$<br>So there are 3 choices for $a$, no matter what $b$ and $c$ are.<br>Hence, there are $3 \cdot 5 \cdot 2=30$ numbers $\overline{a b c}$ that have odd digit $b$ and are divisible by 12.

wajo23_q9.solution:
For $a$ and $b$ to have the same remainder on division by $m, a$ and $b$ must differ by a multiple of $m$.<br>So for two numbers $a$ and $b$ to have the same remainders on division by all the integers from 2 to $7, a$ and $b$ must differ by a common multiple of all the numbers from 2 to 7, namely a multiple of $\operatorname{lcm}(2,3,4,5,6,7)=3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5 \cdot 7=420$.<br>So to find the largest integer less than 1000 that has the same remainders as 2023 when divided by each integer from 2 to 7, we need only subtract multiples of 420 from 2023 until we are less than 1000. Observe that<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
&amp; 2023-2 \cdot 420=1183 \\
&amp; 2023-3 \cdot 420=763.
\end{aligned}
$$<br>So the required number is 763.

wajo23_q12.solution:
There are 50 odd numbers less than 100,5 of which are 1-digit numbers. Hence there are 45 2-digit odd numbers.<br>Similarly, there are 450 3-digit odd numbers.<br>Therefore, of the numbers concatenated:<br>3 are 1-digit numbers, giving: 3 digits 45 are 2-digit numbers, giving: 90 digits 450 are 3-digit numbers, giving: 1350 digits,<br>so that there are<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{4}(2023-(1350+90+3)) &amp; =\frac{1}{4} \cdot 580 \\
&amp; =145
\end{aligned}
$$<br>4-digit numbers, starting at 1001 and finishing at $1000+2 \cdot 145-1=1289$.<br>Thus, the number formed by the 2023-digit number's last 3 digits is 289.
